# Implement digital paper notes and shadows

## Scope and existing evidence

Historical evidence is domain structure, not an operating raster or PDF
renderer; the finite design acceptance is explicitly bounded.

Render loose note fills, folds, stacking, and soft shadows as digital-only
objects with readable contrast and explicit live incompatibility.

Current executable evidence now freezes one digital loose-paper-note plan with
caller-owned fill, fold, stack order, and soft-shadow intent plus
caller-computed readable-contrast evidence. Structural validation requires that
readability evidence without choosing a contrast metric or threshold, and
success now returns constructor-sealed evidence borrowing the exact note plan.
A 64-state style/readability sweep requires direct and sealed admission to
depend only on caller-established readability, never on opaque effect values.

The existing output capability matrix separately keeps loose paper notes, folds,
paper shadows, simulated shadows, and textured fills accepted only for Digital
and rejected for Live.

Contrast measurement, concrete style/geometry values, note layout, occlusion,
shadow rendering, raster composition, and digital preview/export remain open.

## Finite digital-only note, fold, stack and shadow contract

**Inputs:** one accepted page and semantic note identity, declared note
rectangle, optional paper color/fold, stack index, shadow preset and measured
contrast/readability evidence. **Outputs:** a digital layer plan or an exact
refusal; the underlying semantic text/ink vectors remain unchanged.

**One first-release treatment:** use a note rectangle from `(110,40)` to
`(185,95)` mm with muted cream fill, a 3 mm upper-right fold, stack index
`2`, and a shadow displaced `(1,1) mm` with 2 mm blur. The fill and shadow
are clipped to their digital decoration regions, without covering the note's
actual text. `Off` removes fill/fold/shadow decoration only and preserves the
note semantic owner, selected page, original ink and its physical bounds.

**Readability policy:** require caller-provided foreground/background
contrast evidence on the body/annotation text and a proposed minimum 4.5:1
contrast ratio for this single treatment. A shadow cannot be treated as a
source text layer or change its hit-test identity. Color values must be
resolved in the selected light/dark theme before proposing the plan; a stale
contrast measurement is incomplete. Use the same source-linked text vectors
in both the decorated and `Off` previews.

**Worked example:** note `n1` contains text span `t1` (`Reminder`), with
readable contrast 6:1 under the cream fill and a valid 2 mm shadow. In Digital
mode the preview proposes the folded note above page background and below
an unrelated foreground label. Switching to `Off` removes only three
ornaments while `t1` retains the exact same semantic ID and vector position.
The numbers are illustrative and not measured by the current renderer.

**Refusal example:** live single-pen output requests that same fold/shadow,
or contrast evidence is missing after a dark-mode theme switch. The digital
renderer refuses the unsupported mode or unreadable treatment; it may offer
an explicit plain-outline note alternative subject to a separate capability
check. It must never silently send paper folds, raster shadows or extra
colors to the plotter as motion instructions.

**Assumptions and limits:** typed digital note plans, structural contrast
evidence and a matrix rejecting those decorative features for Live exist.
Pixel shadow blur, theme contrast computation, stacking layout, visual
occlusion and digital preview/PDF treatment are not implemented. Theoretical
acceptance defines one bounded ornament, not a working CSS or PDF renderer.

## Acceptance decision

- **Basis:** practical for typed digital-note/Live-mode policy; theoretical
  for colors, shadow geometry and contrast measurement, 2026-11-02.
- **Delivered:** one fold/stack/shadow, digital `Off` switch, source-stable
  note example and Live/contrast refusal.
- **Not implemented by this acceptance:** visible paper simulation,
  theme-aware contrast analysis, note shadow renderer or live alternative.
